  "@type": "sc:Dataset",
  "citeAs": "The UniProt Consortium\nUniProt: the Universal Protein Knowledgebase in 2025\nNucleic Acids Res. 53:D609–D617 (2025)",
  "conditionsOfAccess": "The mission of UniProt is to provide the scientific community with a comprehensive, high-quality and freely accessible resource of protein sequence and functional information.",
  "conformsTo": "http://mlcommons.org/croissant/1.1",
  "datePublished": "2026-06-10",
  "dct:provenance": "Authoritative discovery began at the configured https://www.uniprot.org/help root on 2026-07-27. The JavaScript fallback page was supplemented only with first-party https://rest.uniprot.org/help/* JSON help documents and the official https://ftp.uniprot.org current-release tree; no GitHub or other public repository was used to resolve Croissant metadata or dataset assets. The official Downloads help page recommends FTP for complete datasets and enumerates UniProtKB complete data, taxonomic divisions, reference proteomes, pan proteomes, ID mapping, proteomics mapping, variants, genome tracks, embeddings, UniRef, UniParc, and RDF. These complete current families are represented as cr:FileSet collections with authoritative unique directory contentUrl values and inclusive member patterns; directly inspected sample/schema assets are additional checksum-backed cr:FileObject entries. A byte-for-byte local mirror was not reasonably possible because the current release includes individual files of 110–133 GB and hundreds of approximately 5.5 GB RDF/UniParc partitions. The eight per-residue embedding assets on the official EBI contrib host linked by the UniProt Downloads page are represented in a dedicated FileSet. Previous releases, geographic mirrors, and unbounded user-defined REST query outputs are excluded as duplicate, superseded, or non-finite holdings. The local evidence transfer downloaded 27 first-party files totaling 1,113,581 bytes. Release notes identify release 2026_02 dated 10-Jun-2026. TSV datatype inference sampled no more than five lines. Exact in-band headers were preserved for the bounded UniProtKB and proteomics tables. The two ID-mapping examples have no in-band header; their exact field labels and order were derived from the official leading README schema. The SARS-CoV-2 gzip file exposed only a descriptive preamble within the bounded initial sample, so no unsupported RecordSet was invented for it. XML, RDF/OWL, FASTA, HDF5, compressed bulk files, and recursively partitioned FileSets do not receive RecordSets because a reliable record extraction mapping was not established from the bounded local evidence. No archives were downloaded or represented: .gz assets are compressed single files, not archive containers. No source columns describe geographic places or coordinates, so a Schema.org GeoCoordinates hierarchy is not supported. Name, description, citation, license, and conditionsOfAccess are exact text from first-party live help documents. The access statement says the resource is freely accessible; the license disclaimer also warns that some data may be covered by patents or other rights.",
  "description": "The Universal Protein Resource (UniProt) is a comprehensive resource for protein sequence and annotation data.",
